    "topic": "Binary search trees",
    "sourceType": "notes",
    "sourceName": "Lecture 08 Notes",
    "section": "Balanced vs complete trees",
    "page": 4,
    "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T09:00:00.000Z",
    "content": "A complete tree is filled level by level from left to right without gaps before a new level begins. A balanced tree focuses on keeping subtree heights close so operations remain efficient. A tree can be complete without being height-balanced, and balanced without being complete."
  },
  {
    "id": "cs301-l08-transcript-insertion-shape",
    "institutionId": "onestop-demo",
    "facultyId": "faculty-nk",
    "courseId": "cs-301",
    "courseName": "Data Structures & Algorithms",
    "lectureId": "cs-301-lecture-08",
    "lectureTitle": "Trees, Graphs & Traversals",
    "lectureSequence": 8,
    "topic": "Binary search trees",
    "sourceType": "transcript",
    "sourceName": "Lecture 08 Transcript",
    "section": "Insertion reasoning",
    "timestamp": "42:18",
    "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T09:05:00.000Z",
    "content": "During insertion, completeness is about where the next node is allowed to appear in level order, while balance is about whether repeated insertions make one side much deeper than the other. Balance protects lookup and update cost by limiting height growth."
  },
  {
    "id": "cs301-l08-slides-traversals",
    "institutionId": "onestop-demo",
    "facultyId": "faculty-nk",
    "courseId": "cs-301",
